1. Definitions

"Beneficiary" means a natural or legal person for whose benefit the
Program, or a work based on the Program, processes data about a domain,
system or other asset that this person owns, controls or uses, as part
of a service provided to that person by an Operator, whether directly or
through one or more intermediaries such as resellers, whether paid or
unpaid, and whether or not that person interacts with the Program. An
asset counts as the person's even where the Operator registers, holds or
controls it on the person's behalf.

The following are not Beneficiaries:
  (i)   the Operator itself;
  (ii)  third parties that only appear in the data the Program
        processes, such as organizations that send reports to it, or
        the senders, servers and networks named in those reports;
  (iii) individual employees or users of a Beneficiary or of the
        Operator; the notice under Section 2 reaches them through the
        Beneficiary or the Operator; and
  (iv)  persons that control, are controlled by, or are under common
        control with the Operator ("Affiliates"), such as a parent
        company or subsidiaries served by a group's own IT department.

"Steward" means Marvin Huber, trading as Kaitiaki Huber, Switzerland,
or any successor to whom the copyright in the Program is assigned.

"Operator" means any person who runs the Program, modified or not, or a
work based on the Program, to provide a service to one or more
Beneficiaries. A person who installs, configures or administers the
Program for another person, on that person's systems, to monitor that
person's assets, also provides a service to that person and is an
Operator. A person who only resells or arranges such a service without
running or administering the Program is an intermediary, not an
Operator.

2. Beneficiary Notice and Source Access

If you are an Operator, then, whether or not you have modified the
Program, you must:

  a) inform each Beneficiary, no later than when the Beneficiary agrees
     to the service or when its first domain or other asset is added to
     the service, whether manually or automatically, whichever is
     earlier, that:
       (i)   the service is based on software whose source code is
             freely available, and which the Beneficiary may run
             itself;
       (ii)  that software is licensed under this License, which grants
             the Beneficiary the rights described in it; and
       (iii) where the Corresponding Source can be obtained;

  b) deliver that notice directly to the Beneficiary in a clear and
     conspicuous way, for example in the registration confirmation,
     the service agreement, or the service's user interface. Publishing
     it only in a location the Beneficiary is not directed to is not
     sufficient;

  c) keep that information continuously accessible to the Beneficiary
     for as long as you provide the service to it; and

  d) make the Corresponding Source of the version you actually run,
     including all of your modifications, available to each
     Beneficiary at no charge, through some standard or customary means
     of facilitating copying of software, and keep it current with the
     version you run.

You may not charge a fee for, or make the service conditional on the
Beneficiary waiving, any of the information or rights under this
Section.

If a Beneficiary receives the service through an intermediary, you may
have the intermediary deliver the notice, but you remain responsible
for it reaching the Beneficiary.

This Section applies in addition to Section 13 of the AGPLv3.

